WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.822
Task statement 1.1.

2
00:00:01.822 --> 00:00:06.346
Design and implement agentic loops for autonomous task execution.

3
00:00:06.346 --> 00:00:14.836
A support agent that stops one step early leaves a refund half done, so the loop's exit rule matters more than any prompt.

4
00:00:16.236 --> 00:00:18.478
Here is the whole idea in one line.

5
00:00:18.478 --> 00:00:19.696
An agent is a loop.

6
00:00:19.696 --> 00:00:25.462
Claude asks for a tool, your code runs it, and the result goes back into the conversation.

7
00:00:25.462 --> 00:00:27.768
Then Claude decides what to do next.

8
00:00:27.768 --> 00:00:36.225
It keeps going until Claude itself signals that the job is done, a bit like a chef calling for ingredients until the plate is ready.

9
00:00:37.625 --> 00:00:40.157
This is the loop in about a dozen lines.

10
00:00:40.157 --> 00:00:45.221
Each round we send the whole history plus the tool list and save Claude's reply.

11
00:00:45.221 --> 00:00:47.120
Then we branch on stop reason.

12
00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.639
End turn means finished.

13
00:00:48.639 --> 00:00:52.120
Tool use means run the tools and send the results back.

14
00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:58.703
Anything else, like a reply cut off at max tokens, is not a finished answer, so we handle it on purpose.

15
00:01:00.103 --> 00:01:03.260
Stop reason is the only signal you should trust.

16
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.970
Tool use means keep going.

17
00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:06.285
End turn means stop.

18
00:01:06.285 --> 00:01:11.547
Max tokens means the reply was cut off, so do not treat it as a finished answer.

19
00:01:11.547 --> 00:01:20.689
When Claude asks for several tools at once, send every result back together in one user message, each tagged with its matching tool use ID.

20
00:01:22.089 --> 00:01:24.879
Picture the support agent from scenario one.

21
00:01:24.879 --> 00:01:27.288
A customer asks where their refund is.

22
00:01:27.288 --> 00:01:34.072
Claude looks up the customer, uses that ID to find the order, sees a pending refund, and checks its status.

23
00:01:34.072 --> 00:01:36.481
Nobody wrote that sequence in advance.

24
00:01:36.481 --> 00:01:42.377
Claude chose each step from the last result, and the loop simply kept running until end turn.

25
00:01:43.777 --> 00:01:47.429
Exam questions love loops that end for the wrong reason.

26
00:01:47.429 --> 00:01:55.319
Watch for checks on words like done, an iteration cap used as the main exit, or a rule that says any text means finished.

27
00:01:55.319 --> 00:01:59.623
Claude often writes a sentence and calls a tool in the same reply.

28
00:01:59.623 --> 00:02:03.340
The right answer nearly always comes back to stop reason.

29
00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:05.298
To recap.

30
00:02:05.298 --> 00:02:09.449
Keep looping while stop reason says tool use, and stop on end turn.

31
00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:13.414
Feed every tool result back so the next turn can reason over it.

32
00:02:13.414 --> 00:02:17.317
Let Claude pick the tools instead of scripting a decision tree.

33
00:02:17.317 --> 00:02:22.212
And never let a text check or an iteration cap be the thing that ends the loop.

